Z||000011||burdell1||06-06-99||08:50 AM||||O.k. What is the difference between R12 and R134A? I see that J.C. Whitney has kits to convert them over that seem to include just about everything.<br>
Z||000012||BOILERMAKER||06-06-99||07:49 PM||||R134 is a smaller molecule than R12 with lesser capacity than R12, and isn't banned (thus far) in the US and is cheaper.  The mixing of the two oils can be quite a disaster.<p>Question:  Will a compressor (model no. 501) work on a V6 5speed??  I saw another compressor on a V6 that was model no. 469 and was curious if I could use the other one.<p>Thx
Z||000013||theogre||06-07-99||07:26 AM||||None of the oils used for R12 or R134 are compatible. Mixing mineral, Ester(POE), and/or PAG in any amount will likely wreck the system.<p>As far as changing compressors...... I don't know. 
Z||000014||Phil||06-07-99||11:15 AM||||My hoses from the compressor in the engine copmt. were burnt through so I took them to a hydraulic hose repair shop and he said no problem. Only there is a problem it seems that Gates rubber is a little slow in getting the type 2 air conditioning hose out to market its been on backorder for over a month now. Nobody seem to have any. But when it's available any radiator/AC shop can make or have them made up
Z||000015||BOILERMAKER||06-08-99||08:41 AM||||I just got off of the phone with a "reputable" Fiero mechanic's shop in IL.  I even have the email address if anyone is interested.  They told me that R12 oil and esther oil (for R134) are compatable.  <p>Now ... everything that I have learned about this conversion has told me that both oils shouldn't be mixed no matter what.  This mechanic told me that esther oil is compatable and PAG oil is not.  I don't know what to believe.  I know there are alot of conversion kits out there where all you have to do is evacuate the system and flush it out with this conversion kit.  does anybody have any insight???  He said he has been doing this for 2 years without any problems.  How can I argue that, but it doesn't make sense with everything else that I have learned.<p>Thx
Z||000016||Phil||06-08-99||12:45 PM||||The story I get is the mineral oil from R12 and R134a don't mix but they do tolorate each other and when you retrofit with R134a and ester the mineral oil just finds little nooks and crannys to hide in and stays out of the way of the ester and R134a. Real crude and unscientific example but it kinda works.  
Z||000017||theogre||06-08-99||12:59 PM||||Any problems he knows of yet......... Some of them may simply not have shown up yet, or may have been repaired elsewhere. I’d like to know just whom he gets his information from. I can’t find any documentation anywhere that indicates mixing oils is a good thing. PAG & POE (ester) oils even flat hate each other.<p>There claim to be a few conversion kits with additives and POE (ester) oil that can be simply dropped into the R12 system. There are a number of problems with this approach.<p>1. It's likely the system now has far too much oil in it. Only by draining the compressor, and replacing the ACC/Dryer, can you get the old oil out. The system is designed to work with a specific volume of oil. Simply adding "stabilized" POE to it will likely leave it significantly over filled. Flushing the system with the compressor and ACC/Dryer in place will likely damage both.<p>2. ACC/Dryers made before R134 was introduced likely don't have the correct desiccant in them for use with 134. The desiccant removes both water and acids from the refrigerant. R134 compatible oils are highly sensitive to water. Leaving the old ACC/Dryer in the system is very likely to shorten the life of the system greatly. Leaving any water in the system at all is likely to cause freeze up of that water in the orifice tube.<p>3. R12 systems that old needing refrigerant likely have failing compressor seals. The refrigerant leaked out some place.<p>4. The O rings and other seals used in R12 systems are not of the correct material for R134. Even with conditioners provided in an upgrade kit these systems are all way old and leaks of the smaller R134 molecules are likely unless the seals are replaced. (It’s been found that the hoses are OK because of the way the oils work in the system.) Remember R134 high side pressure is greater than R12, this can also push any marginal seals to failure.<br>
Z||000018||BOILERMAKER||06-08-99||03:46 PM||||Thanks for everyone's help.  I think that I have a direction to go to now.  If all works out, I will let you know.  If all doesn't work out, I will still tell of my mistakes.<p>I have been surfing and found a site that is quite time consuming to go through, but worth the effort.  It doesn't answer all questions, but it does get one on the right track and room to ask more questions if so inclined.<p> <A HREF="http://www.aircondition.com/wwwboard/archive3.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.aircondition.com/wwwboard/archive3.htm</A> 
Z||000019||Cooter||06-17-99||09:40 PM||||For what it's worth- When I bought my Fiero, the air was dead- no charge at all in it. I had it vacumed down, added some kind of oil- I dont know what but the label said it is compatible with r12 and 134- then shot the 134 in it. It has been cooling great for over a year with replacing any hoses, o-rings or anything. Maybe I'm lucky, but last time I checked the system, it had not lost any of the 134.
Z||000020||theogre||06-17-99||11:55 PM||||You may got lucky. The "upgrade kit" with the modified oil may be better than I think. They keep changing that stuff all the time.<p>Personally I'm still pulling the system appart before I push new refrigerant in. At the very least the clutch on the compressor is scragged. I think I'll try one of these VOV units instead of the standard orifice tube. They aren't very expensive, and any reduction in compressor load on a 4 cyl motor is a good thing.
Z||000021||87FieroGT||06-18-99||09:49 AM||||I just did the same thing as Cooter. System cools perfect. As far as leaks I won't know until a little down the road. When I bought the car it had no R12 at all. The mechanic I took it too just evacuated the system, added the new oil, and then added the R134a. What a difference from no AC to crisp cold air; especially here in the Rio Grande Valley. I think that all the fuss about replacing all the O rings and dryer is just a way for the factory to get more money. In some cases it may have to be done due to a fault that was already there, but in mine and Cooter's case and maybe some others out there I don't think it is so. Anyway that is my two cents worth.
Z||000022||BOILERMAKER||06-29-99||08:50 AM||||Have sealed the system last weekend and am going to charge the system this weekend with 134.  So far so good with little work at all on it.  I will let everyone know how it is going when I get it working.
Z||000023||Phil||07-17-99||12:47 PM||||My new hose material finally came in and has been installed. I had already changed the compressor after draining the R12 oil and putting in Ester, also seeing everything was apart I changed all "O" rings and the dryer. Charged it up and it works like gangbusters. Two suggestions for an easier installation: 1 get a pressure gage(Pep Boys $18 measures either low or high sides) 2 put a jumper across the cycling switch on the dryer until you're finished filling.
